.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 107 PM PST Fri Nov 13 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

A cold front is currently moving through the Bay Area and north
Central Coast producing light to moderate, and at times brief heavy
downpours. There remain a slight chance (15% or less) of
thunderstorms across the North Bay, East Bay, South Bay, and the San
Francisco Bay Area through early afternoon. However, there haven`t
been any reports or indications of lightning so far. We have seen
wind gusts up to around 55-65 mph, but those have been mostly in the
peaks across the region and up to 55 mph along the coast. Still
enough to result in downed trees and branches leading to isolated
power outages. Also, have had a few reports of nuisance flooding in
the Santa Cruz Mountains and elsewhere across the region. Not
expecting much more than typically poor drainage areas and minor
roadway flooding across he region. The front will clear the North
Bay soon, the Bay Area by around 3 PM, and the Central Coast by 6
PM. In wake of the frontal passage, expecting post frontal rain
showers to persist this evening and into early Saturday morning.

Tonight, temperatures are forecast to be cooler than the previous
nights. Temperatures are forecast to drop into the lower 40s in the
North Bay and southern Salinas Valley with mid 40s to lower 50s
elsewhere. The coldest wind sheltered valleys potentially will be as
cold as the upper 30s Saturday morning.

Saturday, we are expecting mostly dry conditions across the region
with partly to mostly cloudy skies. However, temperatures will
struggle to warm to the mid 60s in the lower elevations while the
peaks top out in the upper 40s to 50s.

Please check the BEACHES section of the AFD for safety tips and more
information on the Beach Hazards Statement/High Surf Warning and
Coastal Flood Advisory.

