.LONG TERM...
Issued at 107 PM PST Fri Nov 13 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

A second cold front is forecast to move through the region on
Sunday, first across the North Bay and then through the remainder of
the Bay Area and Central Coast in the afternoon and evening hours.
Currently, rainfall totals are forecast to be similar to today`s
frontal passage. In wake of that front, Tuesday morning`s minimum
temperatures are forecast to be in 30s across the North Bay Valleys,
southern Salinas Valley, and 40s elsewhere. Will need to monitor for
cold weather products.

From previous forecaster: "Another lull in the rainfall comes later
Monday into Tuesday, before a third system comes into the region
later on Tuesday through Wednesday and Thursday. It`s a little too
early at this stage to be confident on how much rain this third
system will bring.

As we go through the week, the main point to watch will be soil
saturation across the region. The initial rounds of rain will be
generally beneficial, reducing our fire weather threat and
recharging the snowpack across the Sierra Nevada. Flooding concerns
will be minimal with nuisance flooding possible across urban areas
and small streams. Outside of these areas, rainwater is absorbed
into the ground, which at this time, remains rather dry. As the rain
continues to arrive, local soils saturate and are no longer able to
hold as much additional rainfall, thus diverting excess water onto
the surface and streams and rivers. Wet soils will also increase the
chance for shallow landslides across the Bay Area, leading to
further damage to homes and properties. Beyond the seven-day
outlook, the pattern remains active with a lean towards rainfall
totals above the seasonal average through late November. We will
need to monitor what this will mean for soil saturation and the
possibility for landslides through the next week."

