.BEACHES...
Issued at 1200 AM PST Fri Nov 13 2026

Beach Hazards Statement/High Surf Warning:

A Beach Hazards Statement is in effect through 6 PM tonight, at
which point it gets replaced with a High Surf Warning through 4 AM
Sunday. A series of hurricane force low pressure systems will
impact the NE Pacific through early next week. These storms will
produce very high NW swell that will impact the coast with very
hazardous conditions. Initially the forerunners will bring strong
rip currents and sneaker waves through the day, while the
breaking wave amplitude builds to 15- 20 feet. As the peak of the
swell arrives on Saturday, breaking waves will reach up to 35
feet. Occasional sets of 40-50 ft are likely at favored
breakpoints. These waves bring tremendous power that will
overpower anyone in the water and cause coastal erosion. The risk
of drowning is very high this weekend. Do not go in the water,
avoid climbing on the rocks or leaning over seawalls, and never
turn your back on the ocean. Wave amplitude will abate somewhat on
Sunday before a new round of very high swell arrives Monday.

Coastal Flood Advisory (Today):

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion from
warmer than normal water will result in tidal flooding concerns
across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco, San Pablo, and
Monterey Bays. At the San Francisco tide gauge, high tide is
expected to be 1.5 ft above normal (7.3 ft MLLW) at 12:49 PM
Friday 11/13. These predictions include up to 1.8 ft of surge that
will enhance the astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide
varies up to 90 minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast
and through the San Francisco Bay, respectively. In addition,
long- period swell will create hazardous conditions along the
Pacific coast beaches with an increased risk of sneaker waves and
rip currents.

Coastal Flood Advisory (Saturday):

The swell on Saturday brings a different variety of coastal flooding
as very large breaking waves pile up along the Pacific coast.
Research shows wave run-up increases the mean water level by 10-20%
of the breaking wave height across exposed beaches. This higher
water elevation sets the stage for large waves to crash over sea
walls and run up beaches onto adjacent infrastructure, particularly
during high tide.

